course: dictionary
lesson: 15859
title: Words that make things look worse
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unflattering means making someone or something look worse than they really are. Flattering things make people look good, so unflattering is the opposite. Look at the start of the word: un- means not.
explain: «The flash made her eyes shut and her face look puffy. It was an unflattering photo.» The picture is real, but it shows her at a bad moment, so it makes her look worse than she usually does. Ask yourself: is this how the person really looks, or just one bad moment?
explain: Words can be unflattering too. «The review said the food was cold, the service was slow and nobody said sorry.» Every part is honest, but nothing is kind. An unflattering review points out problems without any praise.
explain: Be careful with news pictures. A newspaper can choose an unflattering picture of a politician to make them look bad. This is a kind of bias, because the choice of picture pushes you towards one opinion. Good readers ask: why did they pick this picture?
fact: Unflattering means making someone or something look or seem worse than they really are.
fact: Un- means not, and flattering means making something look good, so unflattering is the opposite.
fact: «An unflattering photo» might show someone with a funny face or in bad light.
fact: Unflattering words are honest but not kind or praising.
fact: Newspapers can choose unflattering pictures to make someone look bad, which is a form of bias.
exercise: «The bright flash made Mia's face look pale and puffy in the picture, which was unflattering.» What does unflattering mean here? | *Making her look worse than she really is | Making her look like a film star | Making her look taller | Making her look younger
exercise: What kind of review is unflattering? | A review written in a foreign language | A review with lots of pictures | A review full of praise and kind words | *An honest review that points out problems without praise
exercise: What does the un- in unflattering mean? | very | again | before | *not
test: «The newspaper printed an unflattering picture of the mayor with his eyes shut.» Why might the paper choose it? | *To make the mayor look bad | To show the mayor's new car | To make the mayor look happy | To show what the weather was like that day
test: Which of these is an unflattering review? | The food was wonderful and the staff were lovely. | We had a lovely day out. | The room was big and the view was great. | *The food was cold, the service was slow, and nobody said sorry.
exam: Which sentence describes an unflattering photo? | The photo showed him holding a new bike. | *The photo showed him with a red face, food on his chin and his hair sticking up. | The photo showed him smiling in the sunshine with his friends. | The photo showed him reading a book in the library.
